tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47516:     "note": "ppcrldimimask [HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5] closed by #527's closure-records commit and owes the REGRESS review, a distinct phase under the regress-is-fable-only directive, batched to the flagship sign-off.  The question is in the row's hold_question."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47523:    "note": "CLOSED by its closure-records commit (2026-10-18): #527 (7e39a49e, 2026-09-12) stopped X(rldimi)'s rotate loop from consuming its own shift count -- the post-decrement had left sh == -1, so every insert mask was built to bit 63 -- and made the mask walk circular.  Detector: regress/ppc_rldimi_probe.py, 29 rows, wired into gate 13 (gate_ppc.sh, pin 29; PASS 110 checks at the time, regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth/gate_ppc_20260912.log).  Kill table 3 (regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth/killtable3_20260912/SCORES.txt): the fix 29/29, the pre-fix HEAD leg 17/29, the identity body 13/29, all nine D_ mutants killed (the joint table: 17 legs, zero escapes).  The repair chain CLOSED on the §F2 adjudication pass by Fable 5.1's ruling (regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/fable_adjudication_20260912.md: zero floor breaches, denominator named).  This row CARRIED #527's five residuals by the 2026-10-02 disposition (regress/artifacts/idlessaudit_20261002/AGY_FLAGS_DISPOSITION.md: 'the carrier owes its recorded work whenever its round runs'); they move to `ppc527residual` (LOW) in this commit, so no named work closes with the row."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47525:   "hold_question": "REGRESS (held 2026-10-18 at the #527 closure): do regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py and regress/ppc_rldimi_probe.py still score 28/28 and 29/29 with CONTROL=OK on a build of HEAD, and 14/28 and 17/29 on a build of 7e39a49e's parent eb26590b -- kill table 3's BASE and HEAD legs (killtable3_20260912/SCORES.txt)?  eb26590b (#526) changed no PowerPC source, so its cpu_ppc_instr.c is the pre-#527 file table 3's HEAD leg measured.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47534:   "note": "*** 2026-09-12, codex farm: the promotion was NOT done -- regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py was a byte-identical copy of the witness (cmp).  Promoted per codex's design and REVIEW (regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth/codex_promote*_20260912.txt, codex_promoreview_20260912.txt; ppcrldimimask/codex_promote_20260912.txt): regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py (28 rows) with RESULT/CONTROL/DONE lines, a private loader stub, census wants pinned to MEASURED literals (they had been derived from the lists they count), and target-instruction CONTROL rows (without them a no-op instruction still read CONTROL=OK).  gate_ppc.sh sections designed to run BEFORE the NetBSD prerequisite with a strict parse -- PARKED at _scratchpad/gate_ppc.ppcround.sh (two new pins would raise SCORE_CLAIMS 25 -> 27) and MANIFEST entries parked (_scratchpad/ppc_manifest.py apply).  ALL UNMEASURED.  OWED: run both, re-table against frozen copies (rldimi M_NOCIRC by name), gate negative controls. *** [2026-10-18, at the #527 closure (Grok 4.7): DONE before #527 committed -- kill table 3 (killtable3_20260912/), the gate log (gate_ppc_20260912.log, PASS 110 checks) and the gate's eleven negative-control cases, ten expected to FAIL and one GOOD baseline (gate_negctl_20260912.out).  History, not open debt.]\n\nFILED 2026-09-10 from the [[ppcrldiclzero]] witness's sibling audit.  MEASURED, not read.\n\nThe handler takes its operands as MODE_uint_t and then rotates with a shift pair using 32-sh.  cpu_ppc.c defines DYNTRANS_DUALMODE_32, so the file is compiled TWICE and MODE_uint_t is **uint64_t** in the 64-bit build.  The architecture specifies a 32-bit rotate over the LOW half of the source -- **the high half plays no part whatsoever** -- but the right-shift pulls bits out of the high half straight into the low 32 bits, which is exactly where the mask lives.\n\nMEASURED, source 0xAAAAAAAA0000000F, destination seeded 0x5A5A..., shift 4, full mask:\n    got 0x5a5a5a5aaaaaaaf0    want 0x5a5a5a5a000000f0\nThe 0xAAAAAA is high-half data the architecture says cannot reach there.\n\nATTRIBUTION IS PINNED BY TWO COMPANION ROWS: rlwinm on the SAME source and shift is CORRECT, because it casts to uint32_t before rotating, and rlwimi at SH=0 is correct because the ternary short-circuits the rotate entirely.  **So the defect is the missing truncation in this one handler, not the shape of the rotate expression.**\n\n*A row testing this is VACUOUS unless the source's two halves DIFFER: the seat's own first draft used an all-ones source, under which a 64-bit rotate and a true 32-bit one agree bit for bit, and it corrected the row rather than shipping it green.*\n\nADJACENT, NOT A DEFECT BUT WORTH RECORDING so a round does not rediscover it: rldic, rldcl and rldcr are NOT IMPLEMENTED AT ALL -- the decoder's default swallows them.  rldic in particular is documented and compiler-emitted.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10: THE WIDTH PREMISE HOLDS AND **TRUNCATION ALONE IS NOT THE FIX**. ***\n\nCasting the source to `uint32_t' before the rotate is necessary and not sufficient.  The prescribed repair splits the combined declaration -- `uint32_t tmp = (uint32_t)reg(...)' with the destination kept at the mode-dependent width -- **and additionally handles the WRAPPING mask**, because the manual's mask wraps when the beginning exceeds the end: `if (mb > me) ra = (ra & 0xffffffffULL) | ((uint64_t)tmp << 32);' AFTER the rotation and BEFORE the existing mask loop.\n\n*** AND A NEW FINDING THAT CONTRADICTS THE EARLIER SIBLING AUDIT: `rlwinm' AND `rlwnm' ARE NOT UNIVERSALLY CORRECT. ***  The witness measured them green on the cases it drove and the audit recorded them as correct; the design review says that is true of those cases and not of the handlers.  **A measured-green sample is not a proof of correctness**, which is this project's own reading-versus- measurement distinction cutting the other way for once -- the measurement was right about what it measured and the GENERALISATION from it was mine.  Re-check both before either is called clean, and do not let this row's fix be copied to them on the strength of the audit.\n\n*** THE GATE CLAIM WAS WRONG AND IS CORRECTED HERE.  I WROTE IT INTO THREE ROWS, A CHANGELOG BLOCK AND TWO COMMIT MESSAGES BEFORE A SEAT CHECKED IT (wiringcliff/codex_ppcgate_20260910.txt). ***\n\n**I CLAIMED: 'no gate in this tree instantiates a 64-bit PowerPC CPU at all, so the whole 64-bit-only instruction group is invisible BY CONSTRUCTION.'  THE FIRST HALF IS FALSE.**  Gate 9 (gate_asan_sweep.sh) enumerates EVERY advertised machine type and subtype -- including bareppc, testppc and macppc/g5, all of which default to the PPC970 -- so 64-bit PowerPC CPUs ARE constructed by a gate today, and have been all along.\n\n**THE CORRECT AND NARROWER FINDING: NO GATE CHECKS THEIR INSTRUCTION RESULTS.**  Gate 9 is a CONSTRUCTION check with no PowerPC result oracle; the two PowerPC gates both drive `macppc -e g4', an MPC7400, which is 32-bit, and their probes select it EXPLICITLY rather than by default.  So the defects were invisible because nothing EXECUTES these instructions and inspects the answer -- not because nothing builds the CPU.\n\n*** THAT DISTINCTION IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN WITNESS LADDER, ONE LEVEL UP, AND I WALKED PAST IT. ***  The ladder's whole rung-2 clause says machine CONSTRUCTION proves presence and NOT reachability, and is a reproduction only for defects that construction itself executes.  I applied that rule carefully to witnesses all day and then made exactly the error it warns about when reasoning about GATES.  A gate that constructs a CPU is a rung-2 gate; these defects need a rung-3 one.\n\nTHE PRESCRIBED CLOSURE IS SMALLER THAN MY FRAMING SUGGESTED: wire the existing 19-row regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py into gate_ppc.sh, asserting its score AND its completion sentinel.  That closes the missing instruction-result check.  **It does NOT close all 64-bit PowerPC coverage**, and the seat says so explicitly rather than letting the round claim it does.\n\n*** DESIGNED 2026-09-10 (ppcrldimimask/codex_repair_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, ZERO cyber flags), AND IT FOUND A SECOND DEFECT AT THIS SITE THAT I HAD NOT SEEN.  MY 'ONE-TOKEN CAST' PREMISE IS REFUTED. ***\n\nThe width contamination is confirmed exactly as filed: for `1 <= SH <= 31' the erroneous expression's low word is `correct_rot32(L, SH) | ((H << SH) & 0xffffffff)', and the existing low-word mask can select those bits.  `SH == 0' bypasses the shift expression and is clean.\n\n*** THE SECOND DEFECT: WHEN `MB > ME' THE ARCHITECTURAL MASK WRAPS ACROSS THE 64-BIT BOUNDARY AND COVERS THE ENTIRE HIGH HALF, so RA's high 32 bits must be REPLACED with the rotated word -- and this implementation preserves them unconditionally. ***  `ROTL32(x, y)' is defined as rotating `x || x', the low word DUPLICATED (`ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt:17143'), and the rlwimi RTL at `:29989' combines that with `MASK(MB+32, ME+32)'.  The wrapping mask visits `MB+32..63, 0..ME+32' -- which includes every architectural position `0..31'.\n\n    MB <= ME   high 32 mask bits all ZERO  -> preserve old RA      (what the code does)\n    MB >  ME   high 32 mask bits all ONE   -> replace with rot word (what the code never does)\n\n**So the repair is narrowing AND a high-word insertion**, and it must sit after the rotation and before the loop mutates `mb':\n\n        uint32_t tmp = (uint32_t)reg(ic->arg[0]);\n        MODE_uint_t ra = reg(ic->arg[1]);\n        ...\n        tmp = sh ? ((tmp << sh) | (tmp >> (32-sh))) : tmp;\n        /*  A wrapping MASK(MB+32, ME+32) includes the high word.  */\n        if (mb > me)\n                ra = (ra & 0xffffffffULL) | ((uint64_t)tmp << 32);\n\nIn the 32-bit compilation the conversion back to `MODE_uint_t' discards that high half, so the existing behaviour there is unchanged.\n\nMUTANT, one character: `>' -> `<' in the new condition.  `rlwimi r4,r3,0,31,0' = **0x506407c0**, `r3 = 0xaaaaaaaa0000000f', `r4 = 0x5a5a5a5a5a5a5a5a' gives correct `0x0000000f5a5a5a5b' and mutant `0x5a5a5a5a5a5a5a5b' -- the low-word insertion succeeds and the high-word replacement is skipped.\n\nTERMINATION PROVED rather than assumed: both fields are masked `& 31', `mb' is reset to 0 on reaching 32, positions are `(b+k) mod 32', and ME is processed after `((ME-b+32) % 32) + 1' bodies -- between 1 and 32.  Every shift count `31-mb' is in range.\n\nROUND SCOPE: **TWO rounds, not one**, with [[ppcrldimimask]].  Same file and family, but one repairs operand lifetime and a circular inclusive traversal while the other repairs source width and a missing high-word insertion, and validating the second needs word-duplication and high-half expectations that lie outside the doubleword detector's contract.\n\nValidated offline over **655,360 cases** (every SH/MB/ME at both widths x ten operand pairs).  **Not compiled** -- the seat had no native compiler and WSL returned `E_ACCESSDENIED'.\n\n**BLOCKER PARTLY CLEARED 2026-09-10:** [[ppcwitnessmaskoracle]]'s `want_rldimi' is corrected -- it was returning an all-zero mask for **2016 of 4096** (MB,SH) pairs, and the manual's own `:6882' (*\"It is not possible to specify an all-zero mask\"*) makes that a flat contradiction rather than a disputed reading.  `mask32' is deliberately left alone pending [[ppcrlwhighhalf]].  A wrapping-encoding row measured RED on the committed build is still owed before either repair is scored.\n\n**THE HIGH-HALF INSERTION HALF IS CONFIRMED OWED, 2026-09-10.**  It was filed as depending on the [[ppcrlwhighhalf]] dispute; that dispute is now RESOLVED by PowerPC Book I v2.01, which states the wrapping MASK and makes no upper-half clearing claim.  So this round needs BOTH halves -- the source narrowing AND the `if (mb > me)` high-word insertion -- and the narrowing alone is not the instruction's repair.\n\n**THE ORACLE BLOCKER IS CLEARED, 2026-09-11.**  [[ppcwitnessmaskoracle]]'s two corrected helpers were verified against PowerPC Book I -- endpoints, wrap direction, the `+32` offsets, the `& 63`, the all-zero-mask assertion's normative standing, and both partition counts.  **PASS.**  A measuring seat can now score this round without the oracle being the thing that is wrong.\n\n*** CHECKED FOR THE INERT-REPAIR DEFECT THAT KILLED THE SIBLING DESIGN, 2026-09-11: THIS ONE IS SAFE, AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN LUCKY. ***\n\n`ppcrlwhighhalf`'s adopted repair turned out INERT because the DECODER mutates `mb` to equal `me` before the handler selection that tests `mb > me`.  The same question had to be asked here, since the proposed repair uses the identical guard.\n\n**IT IS FINE, and the difference is WHERE `mb` COMES FROM:**\n\n        X(rlwimi):  int mb = (iword >> 6) & 31;        <-- decoded FRESH, in the handler\n                    tmp = sh ? (rotate) : tmp;\n                    if (mb > me) ...                   <-- the proposed insertion, HERE\n                    for (;;) { ... mb ++; ... }        <-- mutation happens AFTER\n\n**`X(rlwimi)` does not consume a decoder-precomputed mask at all -- it decodes `mb` and `me` from `ic->arg[2]` itself -- so it never inherits the mutated value.**  `rlwinm` and `rlwnm` DO consume a precomputed mask, and their handler selection happens in the decoder, downstream of the loop that walks `mb` to `me`.\n\n*So the two rounds are not two instances of one hazard: one repair sits upstream of its own mutation and the other sat downstream of someone else's.  Recording the reason rather than only the verdict, because a future round asking \"is my guard inert?\" needs the discriminator -- **does this code decode the value it tests, or receive it?** -- and not a list of which handlers happened to be safe.*\n\n*** MEASURED AND REPAIRED 2026-09-11 BY agy ON **GEMINI 3.8 FLASH (HIGH)** -- the first round on the 3.8 seat, switched and liveness-verified the same hour. ***\n\n**WITNESS IS TEST-FIRST AND THE ORDER IS PROVEN BY MTIME, not asserted:** `witness_committed.out` 00:46, `candidate_fix.diff` 00:47, `witness_fix.out` 00:48.\n\n    committed build   14/28   -- 93 census cells differing from the architectural result\n    repaired build    28/28   -- 0 census cells differing, no failing rows\n\n**THE DIFF IS THE DESIGNED TWO-PART REPAIR, BOTH HALVES:** `uint32_t tmp = (uint32_t)reg(...)` splitting the declaration so `ra` keeps `MODE_uint_t`, and the `if (mb > me)` high-word insertion placed AFTER the rotate and BEFORE the loop -- which is the placement that keeps it from being inert, since the loop walks `mb` to `me`.\n\n*** AND IT FOLDED IN THE AXIS I HAD NOT ENUMERATED, WITHOUT BEING TOLD TO. ***  The pre-fix failing set includes **`alias nowrap`, `alias wrap`, `alias both`** -- REGISTER ALIASING, which a review named to me yesterday as an axis my four (mb, me, shift, compilation width) did not cover, and which I passed to this seat only as an open question.  **It rowed it.**  So `RA == RS` is measurably affected pre-fix and correct post-fix.\n\nThe rest of the pre-fix failing set spans both defects and both mask classes, which is what the brief demanded: three non-wrapping rows, four wrapping rows, `wrap fullword`, the two preservation rows, and the census.\n\n**STILL RUNNING when the print budget expired: the kill table** (`killtable_20260911/`).  Same orphan shape as this seat's earlier round today -- *\"root agent idle; waiting for 1 background task(s)\"* -- but the brief told it to write its report to disk as it goes, so the artifacts are accumulating rather than being held for a final flush.  **A 120-minute print budget was not enough either; the lesson is to collect from the artifacts directory, not from the seat's stdout.**\n\n*** THE KILL TABLE IS COMPLETE AND STRONG, AND ITS `NULL_MEDIUM` LEG IS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL.  Found by opening the mutator rather than reading the leg name. ***\n\n    BASE          28/28        M_ONLY_HIGH   19/28\n    M_REV_COND    13/28        M_POST_LOOP   19/28\n    M_ONLY_CAST   19/28        M_ORIG        14/28   (= HEAD, identical failing set)\n    HEAD          14/28        RESTORED      28/28\n    NULL_MEDIUM   28/28        <-- SEE BELOW\n\nClearance: **Part 1 (cmp vs SEC) CLEAN, Part 2 (binary -nt source) FRESH, sentinel cleared.**\n\n**THREE LEGS EARN THEIR PLACE AND TWO OF THEM ANSWER QUESTIONS I ASKED EXPLICITLY:**\n  * **`M_ONLY_CAST` 19/28 and `M_ONLY_HIGH` 19/28** -- each half of the repair ALONE is killed, so **neither half suffices** and the two-part claim is measured rather than asserted.\n  * *** `M_POST_LOOP` 19/28 -- the INERT-PLACEMENT mutant. *** Moving the conditional after the loop is KILLED, which is the proof-by-measurement I asked for that the placement is load-bearing.  **That is the exact defect that made the sibling `rlwinm` design inert, and here it is a dead mutant instead of a shipped mistake.**\n\n*** BUT `NULL_MEDIUM` IS NOT A NULL MEDIUM. ***  The mutator reads `if leg in (\"BASE\", \"NULL_MEDIUM\", \"RESTORED\"): replacement = FIXED_BLOCK` -- **so the leg applies the SAME fixed block as BASE and is BASE under another name.**  It scores 28/28 because it is identical, not because the detector survives a degenerate input.\n\n**VACUITY FLOOR CLAUSE (c) IS THEREFORE UNDISCHARGED**: the detector must FAIL on a stale copy or a comment-only file, proving it carries identity rows and is actually testing the binary it claims.  A leg that cannot fail proves nothing and reads in the table as though it did -- *the check-that-cannot-fail shape, inside a floor clause whose whole purpose is to catch that shape.*\n\n**OWED BEFORE THIS SHIPS:** a real null medium -- run the frozen probe against a STALE binary, or against a source whose only change is a comment -- and show it RED.  Cheap, one leg, and it is the difference between claiming clause (c) and discharging it.\n\n*** 2026-09-11: THE KILL TABLE'S NULL-MEDIUM LEG IS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL, SO VACUITY-FLOOR CLAUSE (c) IS UNDISCHARGED.  L2 -- repaired in-round. ***\n\n`killtable_20260911/mutate_rlwimi.py:179` reads\n\n    if leg in (\"BASE\", \"NULL_MEDIUM\", \"RESTORED\"):\n        replacement = FIXED_BLOCK\n\nso the NULL_MEDIUM leg COMPILES BYTE-IDENTICAL CODE TO BASE.  Its 28/28 in `SCORES.txt` is not a result: all three legs are the same binary, and the leg would report 28/28 whether or not the detector could detect anything.\n\nClause (c) requires the detector to FAIL on a medium carrying none of the behaviour under test.  A leg that recompiles the fix is a third copy of the base leg wearing the clause's name.\n\n**This is the check-that-cannot-fail family arriving INSIDE the floor clause whose whole purpose is to catch vacuity** -- which is worth recording as its own shape.  The carrier's existing instances are a `say` in a shell script and a sentence in a seat table; this one is a kill-table leg, i.e. it sits in the measurement apparatus and renders as a scored row.  A reader of `SCORES.txt` sees four green identity-ish legs and reads three independent confirmations where there is one.\n\nNOTHING ELSE IN THE ROUND IS AFFECTED: the six real mutants each scored a genuine kill (M_REV_COND 13/28, M_ONLY_CAST 19/28, M_ONLY_HIGH 19/28, M_POST_LOOP 19/28, M_ORIG 14/28, HEAD 14/28), and M_POST_LOOP at 19/28 independently proves the conditional's PLACEMENT is load-bearing.  The fix is not in question; the FLOOR CLAIM is.\n\nIN FLIGHT: agy 3.8 is building the real leg -- an identity `X(rlwimi)` body, in a scratch clone, no shared build tree touched -- where every row that genuinely observes rlwimi must RED.  Any row still green under an identity body is vacuous by construction and is the most valuable thing that run can return.  Brief predicts the `census` bookkeeping rows may legitimately survive; that is to be DISCLOSED with its reason, not waived.\n\nSTILL OWED after that: the full panel pass 2 on the diff.  Held rather than fired because a measuring seat's kill table was in flight and panel seats are CPU-heavy -- a deliberate hold, neither a seat failure nor agreement.\n\n*** CORRECTION 2026-09-11: **the existing `regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py` has 24 ROWS, NOT 19.**  THE \"19-ROW\" FIGURE IS WRONG and it appears verbatim in this row and in its sibling. ***\n\nMeasured three ways: `git show e777ff0:regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py | grep -c '^row('` = **24**; HEAD = 24; worktree = 24; and `git log --follow` shows no change since `e777ff0`.  `ppcwitnessmaskoracle` calls the same instrument a **\"24-row detector\"**, so the ledger contradicted itself.\n\n**19 is a SCORE, lifted from different artifacts** -- `regress/artifacts/ppcrldiclzero/witness_committed.out` reads `SCORE 19/27`, and the superseded `pass2_20260910/opus_probe_BASE.raw` reads `SCORE 19/19`.  *A score transcribed into a row-count column is the frozen-number class in a new coat, and this one is load-bearing: it is the prescribed gate-wiring closure, so a future round wiring \"19 rows\" into a gate would pin a denominator five short and the check would pass while testing less than it claims.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PASS 2 (codex GPT-6 Astra at max).  **Both algorithms VERIFIED; THE PROBE COVERS NEITHER FIX, AND THESE ARE TWO COMMITS NOT ONE.** ***\n\n*VERDICT: Both local algorithms check out; mode integration is unverified, and the probe covers neither fix.*\n\n**THE ARITHMETIC IS CONFIRMED, and by exhaustion rather than by argument:** independent in-memory models found **no mismatches across all 4,096 `rldimi` mask combinations and all 1,024 `rlwimi` mask pairs**, plus operand checks covering every word-rotate encoding in BOTH destination widths.  *Stated honestly as arithmetic checks, not compiled-C or emulator runs.*\n\n**`rldimi`: `(64 - sh) & 63` IS CORRECT.**  Book I's RTL is `m <- MASK(b, !n)` with `n` six bits, so `!n` is `63-SH` and the loop must stop once `m` has advanced past it.  `:4315` names that endpoint in prose.  *The pre-fix `63 - sh` read `sh` AFTER `while (sh-- != 0)` had left it at -1.*\n\n=== *** THE FINDING THAT CHANGES THE ROUND: NO DETECTOR COVERS EITHER FIX *** ===\n> *'There is no shared functioning detector here -- the permitted probe detects NEITHER repair.'*\n\n`ppc_rldicl_probe.py`'s two expected-value helpers exist but implement DISTINCT instruction formulas.  **So both fixes would ship with nothing that fails on the pre-fix build** -- vacuity floor clause (a) unmet, for both.  **Each commit owes instruction-specific observations that kill its own effect-nullifying mutant, including a SEPARATE WIDTH-SENSITIVE observation for `rlwimi`.**\n\n*** AND THEY ARE TWO COMMITS, NOT ONE.  The scope rule's join needs SAME SEMANTIC CLASS **AND ONE FIX DESIGN**; the class matches and the designs do not: *** `rldimi` preserves the shift amount and builds a correct inclusive circular mask; `rlwimi` narrows the rotation SOURCE and implements a duplicated high-word insertion.  *Even granting that the existing helpers satisfy 'no new oracle', the one-fix-design half fails.*\n\n=== THE 32-BIT MODE QUESTION: MY WORRY WAS HALF RIGHT, AND THE HALF THAT SURVIVES IS THE SHARPER ONE ===\nThe truncation is **sound as a representation argument, not an accident**: assignment to a 32-bit `MODE_uint_t` discards the upper bits by defined unsigned-conversion semantics, the shift itself happens at 64-bit width, and the loop then performs the low-word insertion.\n\n> *** BUT Book I S1.2 (`:342`): both execution modes affect ALL 64 BITS when an instruction sets a 64-bit register, and the register result is MODE-INDEPENDENT; `:1989` describes every GPR as 64 bits.  **So a 64-bit processor running in 32-bit MODE still owes the wrapping high-word write.** ***\n\n**The permitted files do not define `MODE_uint_t` or `reg`, or establish which implementation selects each compiled variant, so the seat declined to certify it** -- *'If a 64-bit processor's 32-bit execution mode reaches a variant that loses the architectural high word, that remains wrong.'*  **Filed rather than guessed.**\n\n**L4 CORRECTION APPLIED IN PLACE:** the one-line comment *'A wrapping MASK(MB+32, ME+32) includes the high word'* invited the reading that architectural 32-bit mode removes half the register.  Replaced with the seat's more accurate wording plus the open question and its Book I citations.  *This file's idiom is `MODE32` with `#else` (see `extsb`/`extsh`) and carries no `MODE64` conditional, so a future guard would be `#ifndef MODE32` -- recorded so the next round does not invent the opposite convention.*\n\n**STATUS: arithmetic green, DETECTOR OWED for both, and the round splits in two.**  Landing is held by owner ruling until the full panel reports.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47705:     "note": "#527 CLOSURE check: 4 flags -- GPT-6 Astra: the five residuals had a carrier, `ppcrldimimask` (the 2026-10-02 disposition: 'the carrier owes its recorded work whenever its round runs'), so the rows could not close as drafted -- moved to `ppc527residual`; the rldimi title's bit counts (corrected in place); `ppcgateimageskip` stale -- the #527 and #565 blocks run before need_file (closed as discharged); the REGRESS hold needs its queued form (the held regress entry and hold_question this commit adds)."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47713:     "note": "#527 CLOSURE check: 1 flag -- Grok 4.7: the drafted '(17 legs, zero escapes)' is the joint table's count, not a per-family one (reworded).  It found nothing else owed and every drafted fact true -- the commit and its parent, both fixes, the pins, the gate log, table 3's BASE/HEAD/identity scores, and eb26590b changing no PowerPC source -- and named the notes' 'ALL UNMEASURED. OWED' as stale history (annotated).  On the residuals it read Fable's 09-12 'not owed before commit ... file it if not taken'; the 10-02 carrier record, which it did not weigh, decided the move."
