tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47757:    "note": "CLOSED by its closure-records commit (2026-10-18): #527 (7e39a49e, 2026-09-12) made X(rlwimi) rotate the 32-bit word at 32-bit width and write a wrapping mask's high word, as Book I's RTL does.  Detector: regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py, 28 rows, wired into gate 13 (gate_ppc.sh, pin 28; PASS 110 checks at the time, regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth/gate_ppc_20260912.log).  Kill table 3 (regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth/killtable3_20260912/SCORES.txt): the fix 28/28, the pre-fix HEAD leg 14/28, the identity body 12/28, all six R_ mutants killed (the joint table: 17 legs, zero escapes).  The repair chain CLOSED on the §F2 adjudication pass by Fable 5.1's ruling (regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/fable_adjudication_20260912.md: zero floor breaches, denominator named).  #527's five residuals, which the 2026-10-02 disposition made `ppcrldimimask`'s to carry, move to `ppc527residual` (LOW) in this commit."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47759:   "hold_question": "REGRESS (held 2026-10-18 at the #527 closure): do regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py and regress/ppc_rldimi_probe.py still score 28/28 and 29/29 with CONTROL=OK on a build of HEAD, and 14/28 and 17/29 on a build of 7e39a49e's parent eb26590b -- kill table 3's BASE and HEAD legs (killtable3_20260912/SCORES.txt)?  eb26590b (#526) changed no PowerPC source, so its cpu_ppc_instr.c is the pre-#527 file table 3's HEAD leg measured.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47839:   "note": "[2026-10-09] THE ORACLE REPAIR IS COMMITTED ALONE, by owner ruling (Fable 5.1's 7n-3 consequence 2; GPT-6 Astra confirmed with changes, regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/codex_astra_confirm.txt): regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py's mask_range(), corrected want_rldimi() and 64-bit mask32() -- uncommitted since 2026-09-11 and 2026-09-17.  Re-derived 2026-10-09 from BOTH helper versions, lifted by ast from HEAD's and the working file (regress/artifacts/ppcwitnessmaskoracle/enumcheck_20261009.out, script enumcheck_20261009.py.txt): mask32 528 of 528 non-wrapping pairs identical and 496 of 496 wrapping pairs differing only by an all-ones high half with the low word unchanged; the old want_rldimi mask all-zero on 2016 of 4096 (MB, SH) pairs, the new one on none, the 2080 non-wrapping pairs identical; want_rldicl and want_rldicr -- the ONLY helpers that file's rows call -- unchanged.  So NO ROW in that file consumes the corrected helpers, and the carrier's oracle-correction bound (one measured red per oracle a ROW consumes) owes nothing there; the probe now says so in a CONTRACT comment.  The order discriminator for the re-pinned rlwinm mask is ppcrlwhighhalf's rung-3 witness on committed HEAD, regress/artifacts/ppcrlwhighhalf/witness_committed_20261009/.  One definition per semantics: the committed mask32 in ppc_rldicl_probe.py and ppc_rlwimi_probe.py are now the same Book I form.  The same commit corrects the probe's TRUNCATION note, which told a reader to recover the lost 2026-09-11 clause with `git show' -- false, that revision was never committed; the clause is quoted back into the file from the brief that inlined the 2026-09-17 diff.  Stays IN WORK until a later records commit names this commit in `commits' and a Fable regress review rules on closure.  ||  FILED 2026-09-10 from the [[ppcrldimimask]] design review, which named the defect and the line; **both halves were then verified locally by opening the file before this was written.**\n\n`regress/artifacts/ppcrldiclzero/opus_ppc_rldicl_witness_20260910.py' is a COMMITTED artifact of `#522' (`e777ff0').  Two of its model oracles are wrong for WRAPPING masks:\n\n  * `want_rldimi()' computes `m = mask_b_63(mb) & mask_0_e((63 - sh) & 63)' -- the INTERSECTION of `bits mb..63' with `bits 0..63-sh'.  That is the correct mask when `mb <= 63-sh'.  When `mb > 63-sh' **the two ranges are disjoint and the intersection is EMPTY**, so the oracle returns the untouched destination for every wrapping encoding.  Its own docstring shows how it happened: it cites the manual's PROSE (*\"1 bits from bit MB through bit 63 - SH\"*), which describes the non-wrapping reading, rather than the `MASK' definition that wraps.\n  * `mask32()' is documented *\"confined to the low 32 bits\"* and sets only `1 << (31 - b)'.  Its companion `rotl32()' correctly returns the DUPLICATED `(r32 << 32) | r32', so the model has the duplication and then masks it away -- the high half a wrapping word mask requires is never selected.\n\n*** THIS DOES NOT INVALIDATE `#522'. ***  That round is `rldicl', which uses neither oracle, and its 190-cell census and 24-row detector are untouched.  The exposure is FORWARD: [[ppcrldimimask]] and [[ppcrlwimiwidth]] are filed on measurements this witness produced, and **the wrapping encodings are exactly where both repairs do their new work** -- so an uncorrected oracle would score a correct repair as wrong, or agree with a repair that fixes only the non-wrapping half.\n\n**THE SHAPE IS ONE THIS PROJECT HAS NAMED BEFORE: a check that cannot fail.**  A wrapping-mask row scored against `want_rldimi()' compares an implementation that omits the wrap against an oracle that omits the wrap, and passes.  *The oracle and the defect share the same misreading of the same sentence.*\n\nOWED BEFORE EITHER SIBLING ROUND MEASURES ANYTHING: correct both oracles to the wrapping `MASK' definition, and add a row at a wrapping encoding that is RED on the committed build -- the order discriminator, so the correction cannot be mistaken for fitting the oracle to a repair.\n\n*** HALF FIXED 2026-09-10, AND THE MANUAL SUPPLIED A SHARPER PROOF THAN THE REVIEW DID. ***\n\n**`want_rldimi' IS CORRECTED.**  The decisive sentence is not the RTL but the mask generator's own definition at `ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt:6879-6882', verified by opening it rather than taking the seat's citation:\n\n    :6879  \"consists of `1' bits from a start bit, Mstart, through and INCLUDING a stop bit,\n            Mstop ... If Mstart > Mstop, the `1' bits WRAP AROUND from position 63 to position 0\"\n    :6880  if Mstart <= Mstop:  mask[mstart..mstop] = ones\n    :6881  else:                mask[mstart..63] = ones ; mask[0..mstop] = ones\n    :6882  *** \"It is not possible to specify an all-zero mask.\" ***\n\n**THE WRAPPING BRANCH IS A UNION AND THE FILE COMPUTED AN INTERSECTION**, so it produced exactly the all-zero mask `:6882' says cannot exist.  That sentence turns the defect from an argument about readings into a flat contradiction, and it is now an ASSERTION inside the new `mask_range()' helper -- the architecture's own impossibility claim doing duty as a self-check that would have caught this on the day it was written.\n\n*** THE SCALE WAS MUCH LARGER THAN 'wrapping encodings': 2016 OF 4096 (MB,SH) PAIRS -- 49% OF THE SPACE -- YIELDED AN ALL-ZERO MASK. ***  Enumerated, not estimated.  After the correction: ZERO.  And it is CONSERVATIVE -- all **2080** non-wrapping pairs are byte-identical to the old formula, and 2016 + 2080 = 4096, so the two sets partition the space exactly.  At the review's worked encoding (`MB=40, SH=40', Mstop = 23) the old mask is `0x0000000000000000' and the new one is `0xffffff0000ffffff'.\n\n**THE PROSE THAT CAUSED IT IS NAMED IN THE NEW DOCSTRING**, because a wrong reason gets reused: the old code quoted `:29909' (*\"1 bits from bit MB through bit 63 - SH\"*), which is the NON-WRAPPING reading, and `:6879' is the definition that governs.  *Citing the prose beside a formula is how a reader is persuaded the formula matches it.*\n\n*** `mask32' IS DELIBERATELY NOT CHANGED, AND THAT IS RECORDED IN THE FILE ITSELF. ***  Its high-half behaviour is the live dispute in [[ppcrlwhighhalf]] -- the same manual asserts blanket upper-half clearing for rlwinm while its RTL and the `:6878' two-copies definition imply otherwise, and three further PowerPC manuals are unconsulted.  **Changing it would adopt one side of an unsettled reading INSIDE A TEST ORACLE**, which is the worst place to do it; the helper carries a docstring saying so and pointing at the row.\n\nSTILL OWED BEFORE EITHER PowerPC ROUND MEASURES ANYTHING: a witness ROW at a wrapping encoding that is RED on the committed build -- the order discriminator, so the correction cannot be mistaken for fitting an oracle to a repair.  **The correction above is a MODEL fix verified by enumeration against the manual's definition; no emulator was run.**\n\n*** THE HOLD ON `mask32' IS LIFTED, SAME DAY: [[ppcrlwhighhalf]] IS RESOLVED AGAINST IT. ***  `mask32' was deliberately left wrong this morning because changing it would have adopted one side of an open dispute inside a test oracle.  **PowerPC Book I v2.01 -- the manual the 2005 PEM names as defining -- states `rlwinm` as `m <- MASK(MB+32, ME+32)` with MASK explicitly \"wrapping if x > y\", and whose only upper-half clearing sentence (`:4195-4196`) is SCOPED to the non-wrapping programming-note uses, never unconditional as the PEM's `:30065` is.**  So the wrapping reading is the architecture's, `mask32` must cover the high half when `mb > me`, and its docstring's deferral note is now superseded and must be rewritten rather than left standing.\n\n*** BOTH CORRECTIONS VERIFIED AGAINST BOOK I, 2026-09-11 (ppcwitnessmaskoracle/codex_oraclecheck_20260911.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, ZERO cyber flags): **PASS.** ***\n\n*\"Both corrections match Book I v2.01 for the stated input ranges and helper semantics.  The assertion and all reported counts hold.\"*\n\n**WHAT WAS CHECKED, because a bare PASS is worth little:** that `mask_range` implements the architecture's `MASK` with both endpoints inclusive and the wrap in the right direction; that `b == e` yields exactly one bit; that `MASK(MB, 63-SH)` is right for `rldimi` and `(63 - sh) & 63` computes it for all SH; that `mask32(mb, me) = mask_range(mb+32, me+32)` is right for the word forms given `ROTL32`'s duplication; that there is no off-by-one in `mask_b_63`, `mask_0_e`, the `+32` offsets or the `& 63`; and that the partitions **2016 + 2080 = 4096** and **528 + 496 = 1024** hold when derived independently.\n\n*** AND THE ASSERTION IS LEGITIMATE, WHICH WAS THE QUESTION I MOST NEEDED ANSWERED. ***  I made *\"It is not possible to specify an all-zero mask\"* an `assert` inside the helper.  **An assert built on a NON-normative sentence would fire on a legal encoding -- worse than the defect it replaced.**  It holds.\n\n**SO THE PowerPC MEASUREMENT CAN PROCEED ON TRUSTWORTHY ORACLES.**  That was the point of asking before the build rather than after: the wrapping encodings are exactly where both repairs do their new work, so a wrong oracle would have produced numbers that were wrong in the same direction as the repair and LOOKED perfectly consistent.\n\n*** 2026-09-11: THIS ROW NAMES ONLY THE ARTIFACT COPY.  THE LIVE PROBE IS AFFECTED TOO, AND ITS SECOND HELPER IS WRONG IN THE OPPOSITE DIRECTION -- WHICH WOULD SCORE CORRECT CODE **RED**. ***\n\nThe row cites `regress/artifacts/ppcrldiclzero/opus_ppc_rldicl_witness_20260910.py`.  The LIVE probe `regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py` carries the same defect at `:158-171` and is named nowhere: `mask32()` is low-32-confined, `rotl32()` at `:150-155` correctly returns `(r32 << 32) | r32`, and `want_rlwinm` then ANDs the duplication away.  Remediation must touch the live probe, not only the artifact.\n\n**AND `want_rlwimi` AT `:174-176` HAS THE IDENTICAL DEFECT WITH OPPOSITE POLARITY, WHICH NOBODY HAD NAMED.**  `m = mask32(mb, me)` is low-32-only, so `ra_val & (~m & M64)` preserves ALL of RA's high half -- which **contradicts the shipped, correct `rlwimi` fix** at `cpu_ppc_instr.c:2621-2622`, where a wrapping mask replaces the high half with the rotated word.\n\n> **So a wrapping `rlwimi` row written against this oracle would score the CORRECT implementation as a FAILURE.**  That is the oracle-correction rule's hazard arriving from the dangerous side: not a detector that misses a defect, but one that manufactures a phantom regression against a fix this project has already measured and shipped.\n\nMITIGATING, and it bounds the exposure rather than excusing it: both helpers are currently UNCALLED in the live probe, and `ppc_rldicl_probe.py` appears in NO gate (`regress/run.sh` GATES list).  **Forward exposure only** -- nothing green today rests on them.  That is why this is filed rather than repaired in-round.\n\nFound by a harvest of unread seat answers, which is the third time this project has found a real defect in an answer nobody had opened.  *Two of the five answers harvested were ZERO BYTES with no quota or wall text -- lost answers, not walls: `wdcaltdigin/codex_impl_20260911.txt` (recovered by a retry that exists) and `ppcrlwhighhalf/codex_redesign_20260911.txt` (NOT recovered, no retry).*\n\n*** 2026-09-11: MY CENTRAL PREMISE WAS FALSE, AND THE CORRECTION MAKES THE POSITION STRONGER. ***\n\nI briefed a seat that the `rlwimi` fix is *\"shipped, measured, correct\"* and that this therefore INVERTS the oracle-correction rule -- code already right, only the oracle moving, so the order discriminator is unavailable.  **`git show HEAD:src/cpus/cpu_ppc_instr.c` has neither repair.**  Both the `uint32_t tmp` cast and the `if (mb > me)` prologue are UNCOMMITTED working-tree edits, and both sibling rows are `in work`.\n\n> **So the ordinary rule applies verbatim and is fully satisfiable: correct the oracle, measure HEAD RED under the new expectations, THEN land the code.**  No special case, no weakened discriminator.  *Asserting the inversion would have thrown away the strongest evidence available.*\n\n**A THIRD WRONG HELPER, AND IT IS THE LARGEST.**  `want_rldimi` in the LIVE probe still computes `mask_b_63(mb) & mask_0_e((63-sh)&63)` -- the INTERSECTION -- where Book I `:4319` is `MASK(b, not n)`, which WRAPS.  **2016 of 4096 (MB,SH) pairs yield the all-zero mask Book I `:4031` declares impossible** (*\"There is no way to specify an all-zero mask\"*).  The witness copy was corrected 2026-09-10; the live probe never was.\n\n**THE SHARPEST NUMBER IN THE WHOLE AUDIT:** old `want_rlwinm` agrees with the shipped `X(rlwinm)` on **131,072 of 131,072 cells** -- every source, every encoding.  It is not *usually* green against `ppcrlwhighhalf`; **it is INCAPABLE of red.**  That is this project's check-that-cannot-fail shape in its purest recorded form, and it sits in a test oracle.\n\nTHE DIFFERENTIAL, with the space corrected: `mask32`'s domain is **1024**, not 4096 -- MB and ME are 5-bit M-form fields (Book I `:4311-4313`).  *The 4096 I gave is `want_rldimi`'s (MB,SH) space transposed onto the wrong helper.*  Non-wrapping 528 pairs IDENTICAL under both (528/528, as required); wrapping 496 pairs DIFFER in exactly the high 32 bits (496/496).  The correction is precisely *add the high half, change nothing else*.\n\n**A NINE-DAY FUSE, and it is the reason this is repaired now rather than filed:** `check_probe_wiring.py` grants this probe an exemption **expiring 2026-09-20**, and `grep -rn 'def mask32' regress/` now returns **TWO live definitions with opposite semantics** -- one committed and wrong, one untracked and right (`regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py:145-148`).  *That is not forward exposure; it is a live contradiction inside one directory with two rounds walking toward it.*  Mitigating and worth recording: the sibling rounds did NOT copy the broken helper -- they independently re-derived the right one three times.  **The trap did not spring by diligence, not by design.**\n\n**REPAIR AS ITS OWN EARLIER COMMIT, NOT AS A RIDER ON EITHER SIBLING ROUND** -- and the reason is the order rule rather than tidiness: *the sibling rounds are the CONSUMERS of this oracle.  An author who corrects the oracle inside the round that consumes it destroys the order discriminator*, because oracle and row land in one commit and no reader can separate *derived from Book I* from *fitted to the repair I just wrote*.\n\n**THE HONEST LIMIT, written down rather than glossed:** for `want_rlwimi` taken ALONE no measurement can distinguish a correct derivation from a fit, because the repair is correct and the corrected oracle agrees with it.  The distinguishing evidence is entirely (i) the SHARED `mask_range` helper -- correcting it simultaneously makes `want_rlwimi` agree with the repair AND makes `want_rlwinm` DISAGREE with shipped code on 47,616 of 131,072 cells (DERIVED by algebra over the enumeration, not an emulator measurement -- labelled 2026-10-09 per 7n-3), a red on a defect filed before this correction, in a handler nobody was fitting to -- and (ii) provenance: `mask_range` was written 2026-09-10 for `want_rldimi`, whose implementation is STILL WRONG [L4 2026-10-09, 7n-3: FALSE since 2026-09-12 -- rldimi was fixed by #527; the helper predates BOTH repairs, which is the stronger provenance], so the helper predates and outlives the repair it is accused of matching.  **A fitted oracle cannot produce a red on a sibling handler it was never fitted to.**\n\n> **STRUCTURAL COROLLARY, not a remark: KEEP ONE MASK HELPER SHARED BY ALL FOUR `want_*`.**  The only way to fit `rlwimi` while staying green on `rlwinm` is to give the two instructions DIFFERENT masks -- which Book I gives identically at `:4127` and `:4317`.  A per-instruction split would be the tell and it would be visible in the diff.\n\n*** CORRECTION 2026-09-11: \"Book I's ONLY upper-half clearing sentence\" IS FALSE.  **THERE ARE TWO.** ***\n\nThe second is at `ppc_uisa_book1_v201.txt:4291-4292`, in the **`rlwnm`** Programming Note (`:4265-4296`), and it is the same sentence: *\"For all the uses given above, the high-order 32 bits of register RA are cleared.\"*  Its three enumerated uses are **also all non-wrapping**, so **the conclusion survives intact and is STRENGTHENED** -- the scoped-to-non-wrapping pattern now holds twice rather than once.\n\n*This row was already corrected once for under-reading this exact span (`38040ac`: \"Book I DOES have an upper-half clearing sentence, five lines past where I stopped reading\").  The word ONLY is that overclaim's residue, and it is a fresh absence claim of the same kind -- an absence claim is only as good as the span you actually read.*\n\n**AND THE DECIDING FACT NOBODY HAD STATED: Book I has NO clearing sentence for `rlwimi` AT ALL**, scoped or otherwise -- its entry (`:4304-4358`) is RTL only.  So the two oracles rest on different strengths of warrant and must not be recorded as one: `rlwimi` rests on RTL with no contrary text anywhere, while `rlwinm` must overcome the PEM's unconditional `:30065`.\n\n**A SENTENCE THAT LOOKS LIKE A COUNTER-TEXT AND IS NOT, uncited anywhere in this project until now:** `:4033-4035`, *\"the mask start and stop positions are always in the low-order 32 bits of the mask.\"*  A skeptic will reach for it to argue `mask32` should stay low-32-confined.  It speaks of the **start and stop POSITIONS** (`MB+32`, `ME+32`, which are indeed 32..63) and says nothing about where the mask's 1-BITS reach; `:4015` says the mask is 64 bits long and `:4026-4028` gives the wrap explicitly.  **Pre-empt it in the docstring** -- it is the strongest-looking objection and costs one sentence to disarm.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- **THE ORACLE IS CORRECTED IN THE LIVE PROBE**, as its own edit, with the enumeration written FIRST. ***\n\n`regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py` gains `mask_range(b, e)` -- the architecture's own MASK, inclusive and wrapping, carrying `assert m != 0` on Book I `:4031` (*\"There is no way to specify an all-zero mask\"*).  `mask32` becomes `mask_range(mb + 32, me + 32)` at 64 bits; `want_rldimi` becomes `mask_range(mb, (63 - sh) & 63)`.  `want_rlwinm` and `want_rlwimi` follow through the shared helper and are not separately edited.\n\n**VERIFIED AGAINST THE LIVE FILE AFTER THE EDIT, not predicted:**\n    mask32   non-wrapping 528  identical 528     (a correction that moved these would be WRONG)\n    mask32   wrapping     496  differ    496     high half 0xffffffff 496, low word kept 496\n    mask_range all-zero over the rldimi space: 0   (was 2016 of 4096, 49.2%)\n    want_rlwimi(SH=0, MB=31, ME=0) = 0x0000000f5a5a5a5b\n\n**That last line is the cross-check that matters: the corrected oracle now returns exactly the answer the MEASURED `rlwimi` repair produces on the wrapping case the round turns on.**  Before the correction it returned `0x5a5a5a5a5a5a5a5b` -- the untouched destination -- and would have scored the correct implementation RED.\n\n**ORDER, and it is the whole defence:** `regress/artifacts/ppcwitnessmaskoracle/ENUMERATION_20260911.txt` was written BEFORE the edit and its mtime proves it.  The enumeration is derived from Book I's definition of MASK rather than from the tree, so no line in it could have been chosen to make a row green.\n\n**SCORE-NEUTRAL BY CALL GRAPH, a static argument needing no run:** all three helpers are UNCALLED in this probe, and the probe is in no gate.  Its 24 rows and their values are untouched.\n\n**AND IT IS ITS OWN EDIT, NOT A RIDER ON EITHER SIBLING ROUND** -- deliberately.  `ppcrldimimask` and `ppcrlwimiwidth` are the CONSUMERS of this oracle; correcting it inside one of them would land oracle and row in a single commit, and no reader could then separate *derived from Book I* from *fitted to the repair I just wrote*.\n\n**THE HONEST LIMIT, restated in the file itself:** for `want_rlwimi` ALONE no measurement distinguishes a correct derivation from a fit.  The evidence is the SHARED helper -- one `mask_range` feeds all four `want_*`, so fitting one instruction while staying green on its sibling would require giving them DIFFERENT masks, which Book I gives identically at `:4127` and `:4317` -- plus provenance: the helper was written 2026-09-10 for `want_rldimi`, **whose implementation is still wrong today** [L4 2026-10-09, 7n-3: FALSE since 2026-09-12 -- rldimi was fixed by #527; the helper predates BOTH repairs], so it predates and outlives the repair it is accused of matching.\n\n*One figure deliberately NOT adopted: a seat reported the corrected `want_rlwinm` disagreeing with the shipped `X(rlwinm)` on 47,616 of 131,072 cells.  That presumes a particular four-source set which I do not have, so the enumeration records it as a seat figure needing its sources rather than as measured.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47938:     "note": "7n-3, for this row: the oracle re-pin is distinguished from a fit ONLY by a measured red on committed HEAD (runnable, since rlwinm/rlwnm are still defective); commit the repair as its own records commit before any handler edit with the contract sentence that no row in that file consumes the corrected helpers and the 47,616 figure labelled DERIVED -- or delete the dead helpers; either way one definition per semantics.  Do NOT mint a shared-helper rule: HEAD already holds several copies and sharing constrains nothing where no sibling row can go red.  'Still wrong today' is an L4 (rldimi was fixed by #527, 2026-09-12)."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47959:   "note": "FILED 2026-09-10 from the [[ppcrldimimask]] design review, which raised it as a limitation on its own repairs rather than as the thing it was asked about.  **Verified locally by opening the function.**\n\n`update_cr0()' branches on `if (cpu->cd.ppc.bits == 64)' and compares `(int64_t)value', else `(int32_t)value'.  `cpu->cd.ppc.bits' is a property of the configured CPU MODEL and does not change while the guest runs.  The manual requires the comparison to follow the current MODE (`ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt:6357'), which on a 64-bit implementation is selected by the MSR SF bit and CAN change at run time.\n\nSo on a 64-bit CPU executing in 32-bit mode, a record-form result whose low word is negative but whose full 64-bit value is positive records GT where the architecture requires LT.\n\n**SCOPE IS THE WHOLE RECORD FORM, NOT ONE INSTRUCTION**: every `_dot' handler in the tree routes through this one function, which is why it is filed on its own rather than inside either PowerPC rotate row.  The seat's own statement of the consequence is the honest bound to carry forward: *those repairs do not establish correct record-form behaviour on a 64-bit CPU operating with SF=0.*\n\nLOW because reachability is unestablished in the sharpest way: it needs a 64-bit PowerPC CPU running with SF clear, and this tree has no gate that executes ANY 64-bit PowerPC instruction and checks the result -- see [[wiringcliff]].  A round here owes the mode plumbing first, and should check whether `cpu->cd.ppc.bits' is consulted this way anywhere else.\n\n*** CORRECTION 2026-09-11: **\"EVERY `_dot` HANDLER IN THE TREE ROUTES THROUGH THIS ONE FUNCTION\" IS FALSE**, and that sentence is this row's stated warrant for filing separately (\"SCOPE IS THE WHOLE RECORD FORM\"). ***\n\n`cpu_ppc_instr.c:205-206`:\n\n    #define FDOT(n) X(n ## _dot) { CHECK_FOR_FPU_EXCEPTION; \\\n            instr(n)(cpu,ic); update_cr1(cpu); }\n\n`grep -c '^FDOT('` = **25 `_dot` handlers routing through `update_cr1()`**, not `update_cr0()`.  The real population is the 33 `DOT0`/`DOT1`/`DOT2` uses plus 4 explicit `update_cr0` call sites.\n\n**The CODE is right** -- floating-point record forms SHOULD set CR1 -- and the defect itself is unaffected.  What is wrong is the SCOPE sentence, and it is wrong in the expensive direction: *a round scoping its work from that sentence would sweep in 25 handlers the defect cannot touch.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:48277:    "note": "CLOSED 2026-10-18 as DISCHARGED, on #527's closure check (GPT-6 Astra): every bareppc-only detector in gate_ppc.sh now runs BEFORE `need_file \"$IMAGES/netbsd401-macppc-GENERIC\"` -- the rlwimi and rldimi blocks, placed there 'ON PURPOSE' by #527 (7e39a49e), and the rldicl block by #565 (8d038908) -- so a missing image no longer turns their coverage into a SKIP.  The sections after need_file load the NetBSD kernel so the macppc machine constructs -- it is never executed; their probe reads the cold debugger -- and so need the image by construction.  Gate 13 on the current tree: PASS 140 (regress/artifacts/ppcrldiclzero/gatewire_20261018/gate13/)."
