tools/pipeline/ledger.json:49962:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/tick_20260917/ (ip30_tick_probe.py, boundary.py, swcontrol.py, tickprobe_run1.out, tickprobe_run2.out, boundary.out, swcontrol.out)",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:49963:     "note": "*** THE TICK ROW IS BUILDABLE AND IS MEASURED: `step 1' KILLS THE HOST.  The codex/agy split is settled BY MEASUREMENT, and BOTH SEATS WERE HALF RIGHT WHILE THE DECISIVE FACT WAS NAMED BY NEITHER. ***  codex was right that stepping alone is insufficient -- the guard must be ARMED (measured: no store + 70000 steps -> alive; bit 49 + 70000 steps -> alive).  agy was right that free-run is NOT needed, but its stated reason -- a 65536-cycle period implying `step 70000' -- is not why the cheap row works.  **THE MECHANISM, verified by me in the source and not taken from the seat:** `machine_add_tickfunction' sets `ticks_till_next[n] = 0' (`machine.c:354') while `ticks_reset_value[n] = 1 << tickshift' (`:355'), and `machine_run()' decrements then fires while `<= 0' (`:727').  **So the FIRST `machine_run()' of any session drives it to -1 and fires EVERY tick function immediately** -- `step 1' executes the store and then runs the tick in that same `machine_run()'.  *** A THIRD FACT NEITHER SEAT NAMED, AND IT IS A FALSE-GREEN TRAP I WOULD HAVE WALKED INTO: **BIT 50 CANNOT BE SET BY `sw'.** ***  `case 0x10000' does `d->imask0 = idata' with `idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)', so a FOUR-BYTE store caps idata below 2^32 -- measured, `sw 0xffffffff' + 70000 steps -> ALIVE.  Arming requires **`sd' = 0xFCE80000** (readback `ld' = 0xDC220000).  **`OP_SW_T0_A3' IS THE ONLY STORE HELPER IN `ip30_abort_probe.py'**, so a tick row copy-pasted from it would arm nothing, stay alive, and SCORE GREEN ON A LIVE HOST DEATH.  **PERIODICITY IS NOT A STARTUP ARTIFACT** -- the boundary was measured from TWO different warm offsets, both sides: warm=10 gives alive at N=65525 and DEAD at 65526; warm=100 gives alive at 65435 and DEAD at 65436.  Both put tick #2 at `machine_run' #65536 exactly, so ticks fire at MR #1, #65536, #131072.  **A guest arming bit 50 at ANY moment dies within 65536 instructions, with no access in flight.**  *The seat's own first derivation said #65537 and its boundary run measured that false -- it recorded the measurement over its derivation, which is the right way round.*  STABILITY: run 1 and run 2 are BYTE-IDENTICAL across all 13 scored rows (9/13, same four FAILs), `IP30TICK_CONTROL=OK` both times.  CONTROLS ALL GREEN, including the two that make the positive attributable: **`tick49 s70k'** (same store, same step count, bit 49 not 50 -> alive) is the discriminator, and **`late s1'** (armed AFTER tick #1 was consumed, one step -> alive) is what proves `tick50 s1' measured THE TICK and not something else about the first instruction.  **L4 APPLIED TO MY OWN PROBE:** `ip30_abort_probe.py`'s `acc imask' row claimed \"a handful of steps cannot reach it\".  MEASURED FALSE.  The row is still safe, for two reasons it did not state (bit 18, and `sw' cannot set bit 50 anyway), and the comment now says so.  **CONSEQUENCE FOR THE ROUND:** codex's constraint is DISCHARGEABLE rather than blocking -- the four-site closure is available because the tick row exists and is measured.  Artifacts at `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/tick_20260917/`, all four carrying their completion sentinel, copied off the volatile scratch path the measuring seat itself flagged."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:49971:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/HEART_reference_20260917.txt",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:49972:     "note": "*** Q3 IS SETTLED BY THE DOCUMENT THE CODE ITSELF CITES, AND THE ANSWER ARRIVED WITH A NEW FINDING. ***  `dev_sgi_ip30.c` says \"according to Linux/IP30\" three times, so the reference is Linux's `arch/mips/include/asm/sgi/heart.h` -- fetched with the owner's approval on this project's vmesbcp2 / 21066 / ddi0406 precedent, then VERIFIED LOCALLY offset by offset before anything was cited.  **Every offset matches the fork exactly**, base included (0x0ff00000 vs `machine_sgi.c:417`). **THE ANSWER: `set_isr` (0x10020) and `clear_isr` (0x10028) are WRITE-SIDE registers.**  The architectural triple is set / clear / status -- write set_isr to set, write clear_isr to clear, READ `isr` for status.  A read of set_isr has NO defined meaning, so **do not mirror `d->isr` there** (that fabricates semantics, which the addition licence forbids) and **DECLINE the read** -- now a TRANSFERRED WARRANT rather than the conservative guess it was when minimax said the packet could not settle it. *** AND IT UPGRADES THE `writeflag` TEST FROM DEFENSIVE TO CORRECT: *** it was argued as a direction guard; the reference makes it ARCHITECTURE, because executing a write-side register's store arm on a READ is wrong on its own terms, independently of the host death. *** NEW FINDING, AND THE CODE ASKED FOR IT IN ITS OWN COMMENT: heart.h documents `isr` (0x10030) as READ-ONLY, and the fork implements a WRITE arm -- `/* Clear-on-write (TODO: is this correct?) */  d->isr &= ~idata;`.  The cited document answers that TODO: NO. ***  Clearing goes through `clear_isr` at 0x10028, which this same file already implements, so the model has TWO clear paths where the architecture has ONE, and the fabricated one is the one carrying the self-doubting comment.  IN SCOPE for this round (same case labels, same function, same detector); it is a member 6 BACKED ACCEPT standing WITHOUT a warrant, which the addition licence does not permit.  Disposition deliberately NOT decided here. **HONEST LIMIT, STATED:** a Linux header is a driver author's model, NOT SGI's specification. It is strong evidence about what the registers ARE and about what this emulator MEANT -- because this emulator cites it -- and it is not a hardware contract.  No claim above depends on the difference: declining a read is safe under either reading.  Preserved at regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/HEART_reference_20260917.txt."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:49981:     "note": "*** FABLE 5.1 ADJUDICATION, 2026-09-17.  IDENTITY VERIFIED -- `claude-fable-5-1`, the nonce, AND the verbatim first line of CHECKPOINT.md, so prompt delivery and file access are proven rather than assumed.  Fired LAST, after all 8 scriptable transports and the Opus measurement. *** **SHIP ORDER: (c) commit the reviewed working set -> (b) run `arm_msrmode_probe.py` -> (a) the ip30 fix as its OWN round.**  Serialisation note it supplied and I had not: (b) runs the emulator and (c) runs a gate, and they contend on CPU but NOT on the tree -- so run (b) to completion, THEN (c)'s gate alone. **A2 -- Q3 IS ALREADY ANSWERED AND THE FIX DOES NOT WAIT.**  DECLINE the read at 0x10020; do not mirror `d->isr`, do not return 0-as-a-value.  *** AND IT RULED ON A SECOND DISPOSITION MY FIVE QUESTIONS NEVER REACHED: DECLINE THE 0x10030 **WRITE** ARM TOO. ***  heart.h documents `isr` as read-only and the fork's clear-on-write is a member-6 BACKED ACCEPT standing without a warrant, which the addition licence does not permit -- Linux/IP30 clears via 0x10028, which the file already implements.  Same function, same case labels, same detector family, so same round.  It owes a STATE row (write declined AND `isr` unchanged), not merely an `alive' row. **A3 -- MY FRAMING WAS WRONG AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART.**  These are not four defects competing to join a round under the reopening rule.  **They are ONE ABANDONED FEATURE** -- the identical `fatal(\"IP30 legacy interrupt rewrite: TODO\"); abort();` at every reachable exit of a rewrite nobody finished.  The unit of work is the FEATURE, not the site, and splitting it would de-fatalise three of four exits and leave the fourth a live host death, which is the de-escalation clause's own hazard.  *** AND IT REFUSED TO LET ME GLOSS THE COST: the 'no new oracle' criterion IS genuinely violated -- the tick needs its own periodicity oracle and its own latch bit, so THE ROUND SHIPS TWO DETECTORS, and the round record must NAME that deviation rather than let a two-probe structure read as an accident. *** **A4 -- IT FOUND MY WEAKEST CORRECTION AND IT IS RIGHT.**  My (iv) said the `acc imask' row is safe *because it writes bit 18 AND because `sw' cannot set bit 50*.  Both clauses are true and **clause (2) MISDIRECTS**: the row's value `1<<18` has no high bits at any width, so width is irrelevant to THIS row -- while 'sw cannot set bit 50' reads as a general safety invariant for imask0, and **the death IS reachable, by `sd 1<<50`**.  *** MY OWN PROBE'S COMMENT IS STRONGER AND MORE HONEST THAN MY SUMMARY OF IT: it calls that row a documented HAZARD and warns that copy-pasting it as a kill row would 'arm NOTHING, stay ALIVE, and score green on a live host death'.  My correction re-labelled a HAZARD as a SAFETY PROPERTY. ***  That is the a-correction-is-not-safer-than-what-it-corrects rule landing on me. **A4(i) -- MY WARRANT OVERCLAIMED TRANSFERABILITY, verified locally:** `dev_sgi_ip32.c` has 6 INTERRUPT_CONNECT/ASSERT sites; **`dev_sgi_ip30.c` has ZERO, and zero `struct interrupt` members.**  'CRIME uses it' proves the IDIOM exists on MIPS; it does not prove member 7 is BUILDABLE for ip30, which has no interrupt topology at all.  The correct statement is *member 7's idiom is available; its application to ip30 requires building the HEART topology and is substantial* -- which is the carrier's own 2026-09-06 lesson (check the member is available on THIS CPU; a precedent real elsewhere is not thereby transferable). **A5 -- THE QUESTION I WAS NOT ASKING, AND IT IS THE RIGHT ONE: DOES `ip30abortkill` ACTUALLY OUTRANK `armmsrmodekill`?**  Rung is a FLOOR ON ADMISSIBILITY; SEVERITY orders the queue, and severity rests on real-guest reachability **that is measured for NEITHER**.  Verified locally: `gate_asan_sweep.sh:593` runs `for s in ip12 ip28 ip30 ip32 ip35` and is GREEN, so **the ISR aborts are on no path anything in-tree travels today**; only one gate names ip30 at all, and the device's own header says it is 'just comprised of hardcoded guesses so far.'  Meanwhile `armmsrmodekill` kills the PROCESS on ARM, whose decode IS exercised across testarm/barearm/cats.  **So the order between the two HIGHs was set without the measurement that would settle it** -- which is itself the argument for running the arm probe BEFORE the larger ip30 build: the cheap measurement informs the order of the expensive work."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:49989:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/FIXDIFF_grok_20260917.txt and FIXDIFF_brief_20260917.md",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:49990:     "note": "*** THE FIX DIFF IS WRITTEN AND PRESERVED, AND IT FOUND A HOLE IN MY OWN DETECTOR. ***  `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/FIXDIFF_grok_20260917.txt`.  NOT APPLIED -- the adjudicated ship order puts the ip30 round LAST, after the commit and the arm probe. The diff honours every ruling: TWO latch bits (`IP30_W_TICK` 1<<0, `IP30_W_ISR` 1<<1, 'one bit per site, never shared'), the `(once)` house marker, `break` and never an early `return` so the common tail at :180-183 still runs, the `writeflag` test at 0x10020/0x10028, and on a READ it DECLINES rather than mirroring -- *'odata stays the function's 0 initializer; that is not a modelled value'*.  It also declines the 0x10030 WRITE arm per the Fable ruling, with the comment *'the TODO answers no.  Clearing goes through clear_isr at 0x10028'*.  It carries a control-flow table showing every path lives. *** THE DETECTOR FINDING, AND IT IS FLOOR-RELEVANT: `kill 30 wr` IS SURVIVE-ONLY AND THEREFORE CANNOT SEE THIS REPAIR. ***  Post-fix the host lives on a 0x10030 write whether or not the store is applied, so an `alive` oracle is invariant under the very edit the row exists to pin.  **That row owes a STATE oracle -- write declined AND `isr` unchanged** -- which is the CHANNEL check's vacuous-by-construction shape, knowable now, before any table runs. **AND THE ISR-UNCHANGED ROW MUST COVER BOTH LOADS, with a twist at 0x10028:** `&= ~0` is a no-op today, so a row that only checks the ISR CELL cannot fail there.  Pin **`odata` is not `d->isr`** instead, seeding ISR to a distinctive value first.  Without that, the 0x10028 writeflag repair ships unpinned."
